tieka316@hotmail.com (Tieka) wrote in message news:<tieka316-ya02408000R0108032040180001@news.mts.net>...
> But when did scumming become bad?

As others have already mentioned Nethack is derived from Rogue, a
UNIX(tm) dungeon crawl game.  The UNIX environment is multi-user and
most of the fun in Rogue was people competing for the top score.  If
the save-games were stored somewhere the user could manipulate them
one could easily hack the score-file.  Thus, in the old days, the
save-game files weren't writable by the users and only the "root" user
could manipulate the files.  (Root == God in UNIX).

(before the unix-wizards jump down my throat -- this is a simplified
explaination and I understand what I'm leaving out)

So originally the only one who could save-scum was Root.  And since no
mortal user could do this no one did.  When roguelikes made it to the
personal computer, where the user has control over all files on the
system, it make it possible for everyone to save-scum.  People who
made it the legit way (w/o scumming) felt those who scummed didn't
perform the same feat -- thus it was seen as cheating.

